 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to increasing\npenalties for leaving the scene of an accident without reporting",
    "basePrintNo": "S9163",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9163-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §600, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9163\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to increasing\npenalties for leaving the scene of an accident without reporting\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo deter hit and run crashes and increase public safety.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill increases the fine range for a first violation\nof leaving the scene of a personal injury crash to $750-$1,000, and for\na repeat violation to $1,000-$3,000.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nLeaving the scene of an accident is a very serious offense and should be\ntreated as such. Hit and run accidents are especially dangerous because\nif someone is hurt and the driver doesn't report it, the victim may not\nget the proper care when they need it. Increasing the fines for such a\nviolation will help enhance public and traffic safety while more proper-\nly reflecting the seriousness of this traffic crime.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: S2349 - Died in Transportation\n\n2017: S2349 - Passed Senate, Died in Assembly\n\n2016: S1726 - Died in Transportation\n\n2015: S1726 - Died in Transportation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of November next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become a law.\n",
